---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  name: "Power BI Date Table Generator",
  url: "https://powerbitutorials.com/tools/date-table/",
  applicationCategory: "DeveloperApplication",
  operatingSystem: "Any",
  offers: { "@type": "Offer", price: "0", priceCurrency: "USD" },
  description:
    "Generates a Power BI date table in DAX or Power Query M with fiscal year, ISO week and sortable month columns.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Date Table Generator"
  description="Generate a contiguous date table for Power BI in DAX or Power Query M: fiscal year, ISO weeks, sortable month columns, and the mark-as-date-table checklist."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/tools/tool.css" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="tool-page">
    <div class="wrap">

      <section class="hero">
        <span class="eyebrow">Free tool · no signup</span>
        <h1>A date table that actually works.</h1>
        <p>
          Contiguous dates, no gaps, a real sort column for every month label,
          ISO week numbers, and fiscal year handled properly. Pick your range and
          copy the DAX or the Power Query code.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="layout">

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head"><span>Settings</span></div>
          <div class="panel-body">
            <div class="field-grid">
              <div class="field">
                <label for="start-year">First year</label>
                <input type="number" id="start-year" value="2023" min="1900" max="2100" step="1" />
              </div>
              <div class="field">
                <label for="end-year">Last year</label>
                <input type="number" id="end-year" value="2026" min="1900" max="2100" step="1" />
                <span class="hint">Always expands to 1 Jan – 31 Dec.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="field">
                <label for="week-start">Week starts on</label>
                <select id="week-start">
                  <option value="monday" selected>Monday</option>
                  <option value="sunday">Sunday</option>
                </select>
              </div>
              <div class="field">
                <label for="fiscal-start">Fiscal year starts in</label>
                <select id="fiscal-start">
                  <option value="1">January</option>
                  <option value="2">February</option>
                  <option value="3">March</option>
                  <option value="4" selected>April</option>
                  <option value="5">May</option>
                  <option value="6">June</option>
                  <option value="7">July</option>
                  <option value="8">August</option>
                  <option value="9">September</option>
                  <option value="10">October</option>
                  <option value="11">November</option>
                  <option value="12">December</option>
                </select>
              </div>
              <div class="field wide" id="fiscal-naming-field">
                <label for="fiscal-naming">Fiscal year is named by</label>
                <select id="fiscal-naming">
                  <option value="end" selected>Its ending year (FY2024 = Apr 2023 – Mar 2024)</option>
                  <option value="start">Its starting year (FY2023 = Apr 2023 – Mar 2024)</option>
                </select>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="checks">
              <label><input type="checkbox" id="iso-week" checked /> ISO week numbers</label>
              <label><input type="checkbox" id="fiscal-cols" checked /> Fiscal year &amp; quarter</label>
              <label><input type="checkbox" id="current-flags" /> “Is current” flags</label>
            </div>
            <p class="note" id="range-note"></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span>Generated code</span>
            <span class="spacer"></span>
            <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
              <button class="tab" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-target="dax" type="button">DAX</button>
              <button class="tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-target="m" type="button">Power Query</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-body">
            <pre class="code-output wrap" id="dax-out"></pre>
            <pre class="code-output wrap" id="m-out" style="display:none"></pre>
            <div class="toolbar" style="margin-top:10px">
              <button class="btn" id="copy-dax" type="button">Copy DAX</button>
              <button class="btn" id="copy-m" type="button">Copy Power Query</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="steps-title">After you paste it</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <strong>Mark it as a date table.</strong> Table tools →
            <em>Mark as date table</em> → choose the <code>Date</code> column.
            Time intelligence will not behave without this.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Set the sort columns.</strong> Select <code>Month</code> →
            <em>Sort by column</em> → <code>Month Number</code>. Same for
            <code>Year Month</code> → <code>Year Month Sort</code>, and
            <code>Month Short</code> → <code>Month Number</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Hide the raw keys.</strong> Hide <code>Month Number</code>,
            <code>Year Month Sort</code>, <code>Quarter Number</code> and
            <code>Fiscal Month Number</code> from the report view.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Relate it.</strong> Create a one-to-many relationship from
            <code>DimDate[Date]</code> to every fact table’s date column.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Never filter it.</strong> Filtering or slicing the date table
            itself breaks time intelligence. Slice the fact table instead.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="cta">
        <div>
          <h3>The date table is step one.</h3>
          <p>
            The free Time Intelligence Starter Pack adds twelve measures on top of
            it — YTD, QTD, YoY, rolling windows — each one explaining the trap it
            avoids.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/resources/time-intelligence-starter/">Get the pack →</a>
      </section>

      <section>
        <details class="faq">
          <summary>DAX table or Power Query table — which should I use?</summary>
          <p>
            Power Query if the table is large, if you want it to refresh with the
            rest of the model, or if you need to join holidays and working-day
            flags later. DAX if you want zero extra queries and the model is already
            small. Both produce the same structure here, so switching later costs
            nothing.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq">
          <summary>Why is the range always whole years?</summary>
          <p>
            Partial years create gaps at the edges and make <code>DATEADD</code> /
            <code>SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR</code> behave inconsistently. A whole-year
            range costs a few hundred rows and removes a whole class of bugs.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq">
          <summary>Why do month names sort alphabetically in my visuals?</summary>
          <p>
            Because a text column sorts by text. Set <em>Sort by column</em> on
            <code>Month</code> to <code>Month Number</code>, and on
            <code>Year Month</code> to <code>Year Month Sort</code>. The generator
            already includes both columns for exactly this reason.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq">
          <summary>Does Power BI’s automatic date table do the same thing?</summary>
          <p>
            It creates hidden tables per date column, which works well enough to
            hide the problem until you add a second fact table or a fiscal
            calendar. An explicit table you control is the version that survives
            those changes — and it is the only one you can mark and relate
            deliberately.
          </p>
        </details>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>

  <script is:inline src="/tools/date-table.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .steps-title { font-size: 1.3rem; letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .steps { font-size: 0.94rem; color: var(--t-text-muted); max-width: 760px; line-height: 1.75; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
</style>
